id: standard-environment
name: Standard Environment
kind: battlefield
environment: true
hardnessMin: 1
hardnessMax: 5
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Solid ground under you. No effects of its own - this is what every other Environment
  is a departure from. Hardness Rank 1 to 5, the ARC's to pick.
text: >
  Standard Environment: The typical environment that most DBU fights will take place in,
  representing a solid floor.

  Hardness Rank: 1~5 (decided by the ARC)

  Effects: N/A

---

# What a character is standing on when nobody has said otherwise, and the one Environment
# that does nothing to them.
#
# A file rather than an empty string, for the same reason the Normal Light Level is a file:
# "no effect" is a rule with a name, and the picker showing it is the picker saying that
# somebody has thought about where this fight is happening. An empty option would read as
# a question nobody answered.
#
# --- The Hardness Rank -------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Hardness Rank: 1~5 (decided by the ARC)."
#
# An Environment has a Hardness Rank the way a Feature does, and it is the same Hardness:
# what the ground costs you to be thrown into it. Sudden Stop already names the two in one
# breath - "Feature Collision or Ground Collision respectively" - and they were the same
# number all along.
#
# A range rather than a number, because it is the ARC's to pick: concrete is not sand and
# neither is a ship's deck. `hardnessMin` and `hardnessMax` are what the picker on the
# Battlefields tab offers, and the Rank chosen sits on the character beside the Environment
# it belongs to.
#
# The Value follows from the Rank as it does everywhere: twice the Rank a base Tier of
# Power, except Rank 0, which is 1(bT). Nothing here stores it.
#
# --- Effects: N/A ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# No statements below the line, so the registry drops the program and this costs nothing to
# gather - exactly what the Normal Light Level does. "N/A" is the whole of what this
# Environment does, and it is worth a file saying so rather than a gap.
